-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Media Gallery Plugin for Geeklog
- development branch: 1.8.0
- based on: 1.7.3
- license: GPLv2
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Status
------
This branch is the active development line for MediaGallery 1.8.0.
Do not deploy it to production without testing and a full backup.

See ROADMAP.md for the implementation plan and compatibility policy.

Goals for 1.8.0
---------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 modernizes the plugin while preserving upgrade safety for existing installations.

Main goals:

* keep compatibility with existing 1.7.x installations;
* support standard single-site Geeklog installations without configuration changes;
* support multisite deployments with per-site media storage;
* migrate remaining administrator preferences away from hard-coded values and into the Geeklog Configuration API;
* modernize templates, JavaScript dependencies, accessibility and SEO where needed;
* retain current bug fixes and improve PHP 8.x compatibility;
* improve interoperability with other Geeklog plugins through public plugin services;
* modernize MediaGallery email notifications using Geeklog-supported templates.

Persistent media storage
------------------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 stores public media below Geeklog's image root instead of inside the replaceable plugin directory.

Standard single-site installations use:

    $_CONF['path_images'] . 'mediagallery/'

with the public URL:

    {site_url}/images/mediagallery

When `$_CONF['images_url']` is explicitly defined, MediaGallery uses it as the public image URL root. This is the recommended multisite setup, for example:

    $_CONF['path_images'] = $_CONF['path'] . 'public_html/images/example/';
    $_CONF['images_url']  = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/example';

which produces:

    .../public_html/images/example/mediagallery/
    https://example.com/images/example/mediagallery

The historical `public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/` directory remains a migration source only. It is no longer the 1.8 runtime storage location. This is necessary because Geeklog's native plugin ZIP uploader replaces the complete `public_html/mediagallery/` directory during an update.

### Mandatory preflight when upgrading 1.7.x with Geeklog's ZIP uploader

Geeklog removes the old plugin public directory before it loads the new plugin upgrade code. Therefore a 1.7.x site that still stores media below `public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/` must migrate those files before uploading the 1.8.0 ZIP.

Extract the 1.8.0 archive on the server and run:

    php tools/migrate-media-storage.php /path/to/geeklog

The tool copies files to the persistent images location, verifies every source file by relative path and size, and deliberately leaves the legacy source untouched. Only after it reports `Migration OK` should the 1.8.0 ZIP be uploaded through Geeklog.

Fresh 1.8.0 installations seed the same persistent storage automatically. Once a site uses `images/.../mediagallery/`, subsequent plugin ZIP updates cannot remove user media.

Configuration
-------------
Recent MediaGallery versions no longer use the historical manually edited plugin config.php file.

MediaGallery 1.8.0 continues this direction. Administrator preferences that remain hard-coded in functions.inc will be reviewed and moved to the Geeklog Configuration API when still relevant.

Derived values such as plugin URLs, template paths and media storage paths should remain runtime values and should not become arbitrary administrator-editable settings.

Obsolete settings related only to legacy technologies such as Flash, QuickTime, Windows Media Player, MooTools or SimpleViewer will be removed where the current code no longer requires them.

Upgrade policy
--------------
Before upgrading:

* back up the Geeklog database;
* back up MediaGallery media files;
* record any local modifications;
* test the upgrade on a staging or local installation first.

The 1.8.0 upgrade preserves existing configuration values, adds only missing Configuration API settings, and migrates legacy media by copy-and-verify semantics without deleting the source.

Existing media directories
--------------------------
Traditional installations use these directories:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/covers
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/disp
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/orig
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/tn

Multisite installations may instead use the same internal MediaGallery layout beneath the site-specific image path, for example:

    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/covers
    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/disp
    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/orig
    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/tn

Templates, accessibility and SEO
--------------------------------
The 1.8.0 work includes an audit of the MediaGallery .thtml templates and associated JavaScript, with current HTML5 markup, responsive output, accessibility, meaningful image alt text, clear heading structure, canonical/stable URLs where needed, continued XML Sitemap compatibility and removal of obsolete dependencies where safe.

Interoperability
----------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 plans to expose supported album information through the Geeklog Plugin API instead of requiring other plugins to query mg_* tables directly.

Email notifications
-------------------
MediaGallery notification emails will be reviewed for migration to Geeklog-supported email templates, including plaintext and HTML variants where supported by the target Geeklog version.

Installable test archives
-------------------------
A manual GitHub Actions workflow named **Build MediaGallery installable archive** is available on this branch.

Run it from the GitHub Actions interface when an online installation package is needed. An optional version label can be supplied; the default is `1.8.0-dev`.

The workflow builds:

    dist/mediagallery-VERSION.zip
    dist/mediagallery-VERSION.zip.sha256

The ZIP contains a single top-level `mediagallery/` directory and excludes repository metadata, GitHub workflow files, generated distribution files, IDE data, local media objects and temporary files.

The generated ZIP and SHA-256 file are published as a GitHub Actions artifact for 14 days. Generated binary archives are not committed to the repository.

Compatibility target
--------------------
The intended compatibility range is Geeklog 2.1.1 through 2.2.2 where practical. PHP 5.6 is included in syntax linting to protect legacy Geeklog 2.1.1 deployments, while PHP 7.4, 8.1 and 8.3 are also checked during development.

For the detailed implementation plan, see ROADMAP.md.
